## 3. Quick Tasks & Explorations

| Command | When to use it |
|---------|---------------|
| `/gsd:quick` | **Small, ad-hoc tasks with GSD guarantees** (atomic commits, STATE.md tracking) but skipping optional agents. Use for typo fixes, config changes, small refactors. Subcommands: `list`, `status <slug>`, `resume <slug>`. Flags: `--full` (full pipeline), `--discuss`, `--research`, `--validate`. |
| `/gsd:fast` | **Trivial tasks inline** — no subagents, no planning overhead. Use for tasks you could describe in one sentence and execute in under 2 minutes. Faster than `/gsd:quick` but without commit tracking. |
| `/gsd:quick-batch` | **Batching several `/gsd:quick`-shaped tasks together.** One coordinator dispatches per-item planner/researcher/checker/executor/verifier. Use when you have 3+ small independent tasks to run in parallel. |
| `/gsd:capture` | **Capturing ideas, tasks, notes, and seeds** to their destination. Modes: default (todo), `--note` (zero-friction idea), `--backlog` (parking lot), `--seed` (forward-looking idea), `--list` (browse todos), `--list-seeds` (audit seeds). |
| `/gsd:explore` | **Open-ended Socratic ideation.** Think through ideas before committing to plans. Use when you're exploring what to build or how to approach a problem. |
| `/gsd:spike` | **Time-boxed technical spike.** Build focused experiments to validate feasibility. Produces verified knowledge for the real build. Use when you need to research a library, test an approach, or validate a technical decision. |
| `/gsd:sketch` | **Sketching UI/design ideas** with throwaway HTML mockups. Produces 2-3 variants. Use before committing to an implementation for any UI feature. |

